WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:09.800
In an adult human at rest, blood leaves the heart in arteries, passes through

2
00:00:09.800 --> 00:00:13.360
arterioles and capillaries, and returns in veins.

3
00:00:14.480 --> 00:00:18.000
It moves fastest in the aorta, the widest artery.

4
00:00:18.840 --> 00:00:22.900
It moves slowest in the capillaries, the narrowest vessels in the body.

5
00:00:23.660 --> 00:00:27.520
That sounds backward, like a river slowing where it narrows.

6
00:00:27.980 --> 00:00:31.080
So why is blood slowest in the smallest vessels?

7
00:00:31.983 --> 00:00:34.143
Two quantities matter here.

8
00:00:35.243 --> 00:00:38.023
Volume flow is how much blood passes a point.

9
00:00:39.043 --> 00:00:41.383
Velocity is how fast the blood moves.

10
00:00:42.403 --> 00:00:47.463
At steady state, every litre that leaves the aorta passes through the capillaries.

11
00:00:48.183 --> 00:00:54.123
So the volume flow through all the capillaries together equals the flow through the aorta.

12
00:00:54.267 --> 00:00:59.027
Velocity equals volume flow divided by cross-sectional area.

13
00:00:59.787 --> 00:01:04.547
The flow is the same, so a larger area means a lower velocity.

14
00:01:05.607 --> 00:01:07.087
Each capillary is tiny.

15
00:01:07.467 --> 00:01:12.967
But there are so many side by side that their combined cross-section far exceeds

16
00:01:12.967 --> 00:01:18.307
the aorta's. Many tiny tubes in parallel add up to a wide channel.

17
00:01:19.150 --> 00:01:23.970
Here are made-up round numbers for an adult human at rest.

18
00:01:25.210 --> 00:01:29.470
5 L/min is about 83 cm³ per second.

19
00:01:30.390 --> 00:01:35.770
Through an aorta of 4 cm², that is about 21 cm/s.

20
00:01:36.830 --> 00:01:43.710
Spread over 3,000 cm² of capillaries, it is about 0.03 cm/s.

21
00:01:44.183 --> 00:01:47.603
That slow passage leaves time for exchange.

22
00:01:48.643 --> 00:01:52.303
A capillary wall is a single layer of endothelial cells.

23
00:01:52.863 --> 00:01:55.543
Little else separates blood from tissue fluid.

24
00:01:56.363 --> 00:01:59.763
Blood pressure inside a capillary pushes fluid out.

25
00:02:00.363 --> 00:02:03.843
In mammals, plasma proteins pull water back in.

26
00:02:04.423 --> 00:02:09.823
The classic textbook model has fluid leaving at the arterial end and returning at

27
00:02:09.823 --> 00:02:14.903
the venous end. Reviews conclude that steady return at the venous end lacks

28
00:02:14.903 --> 00:02:17.403
reproducible support as a general account.

29
00:02:18.203 --> 00:02:22.843
On that view, most tissues filter slowly, and lymph returns the fluid.

30
00:02:23.633 --> 00:02:30.373
Keep one rule. Velocity is volume flow divided by cross-sectional area.

31
00:02:31.273 --> 00:02:36.853
In adult humans, blood is slowest in the capillaries because together they have the

32
00:02:36.853 --> 00:02:38.133
largest cross-section.

33
00:02:39.033 --> 00:02:41.513
Each one being narrow is not the reason.

34
00:02:42.413 --> 00:02:48.873
Many tiny tubes side by side give a wide channel, and a wide channel means slow blood.
